#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087391 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8edbecf-59d3-5f32-a0ac-d6e8f2128dc0" :knowledge-point/title "Transforming a Homogeneous Differential Equation into a Separable Equation Using a Substitution"}] :question/id #uuid "34c357f1-505b-530f-8bb4-376eada248a9" :question/math-academy-id "e-2301" :question/problem "Use the transformation $y = vx$  (where $v=v(x)$) to rewrite the given first-order homogeneous ODE \n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}    =   \\cos \\left( \\frac {y} {x} \\right)\n$$\n\nas a separable ODE in $v$ and $x.$" :question/worked-solution "With $y = vx$ (where $v = v(x)$), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging that into the differential equation and separating variables, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(v) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(v)-v \\\\ (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(v)-v})\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087392 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "49eaf082-07de-5468-a2d7-4cdfce27a553" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Homogeneous Differential Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "89b7276c-ef41-5123-ad31-b54753656111" :question/math-academy-id "e-2302" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044192/assets/12aec388c94c8c4d4f2099b98f5a489d110fa3ef0d58e7895d9a425e49f8eb77.png)" :question/worked-solution "We note that this equation is a homogeneous first-order differential equation because\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044192/assets/c3215663fd1a332f750b8af4049404061e2b1c64726c81572d1a1e76816bb718.png)\n\nThe given equation is not separable, but we can transform it into a separable equation by carrying out the substitution\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044192/assets/7c57d5a7c4003269ba9eacf6240394f508347989a9d75665c7070e31cbdad701.png)\n\nCarrying out the substitution and separating variables, we have\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044192/assets/8ad7a31876bc67fa69ff24ed41badcf6531ddf52b2d1847af4b0b75595ab5bcd.png)\n\nIntegrating both sides, we get\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044192/assets/7a6577d6ac4f3f1fc100a2767d77509fbe211f7d3e7bc7a2bba027080e7e6ccd.png)\nwhere ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044192/assets/4247e7a16afb46979bc6ecfc9ae10dc6c92d0f5f1ff5e1b2a1f412ec86a48a83.png) (with ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044192/assets/9e71f72815a80396d1186bfed1047662e1d507561c7160d1c2d89c2f23421c09.png)) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044192/assets/012dd077bd0f68f82ae37fa50d493e97bd67ac5ff9f301e48ca29e91b1af16ab.png) \n\nFinally, substituting ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044192/assets/f74fcd854fc4be51cd8eed8f4d0649bcc7cf11089a7aa2a7e7edd8d306693f78.png) and simplifying, we get\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044192/assets/05eae130ba10a3386a1c8621ecc3e3d3ec0bb7af352fb4c928cf8bf2d88c21a3.png)\nwhere ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044192/assets/8939a3ee49356a0cc8515cce98d84ecf10279dad9d2f76fac07660d0f99a0d7b.png) Therefore, the general solution is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044192/assets/185e5a72b055183d70fb6d9dbc0227190a8114b80b2dc4193d8f542d5c85c0d7.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087393 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8c2a855-07a3-5855-9f6c-1a914953ac53" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous Differential Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "f34de8cd-b650-5926-9f06-8127d4fb3a55" :question/math-academy-id "e-2393" :question/problem "Which of the following differential equations are homogeneous?\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{y}{x}$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{y}{x^2}$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{y}{2x}\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{x^2}{y^2}\\right)}$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order ordinary differential equation that is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n    =\n    f(x, y)\n$$\n\nis said to be homogeneous if $f(x,y)$ is a homogeneous function of degree zero, meaning that it satisfies\n\n$$\nf(tx, ty) = f(x, y).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at the right-hand side function in each differential equation.\n\n- The function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{y}{x}$ is a homogeneous function of degree zero:\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(ty)}{(tx)} \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\frac{t\\cdot y}{t\\cdot x} \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\  & =f(x,y)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, equation I is a homogenous differential equation.\n\n- The function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{y}{x^2}$ is *not* a homogeneous function of degree $0.$ Rather, it is a homogeneous function of degree $-1\\mathbin{:}$\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(ty)}{(tx)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\frac{t\\cdot y}{{t}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot \\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot f(x,y) \\\\  & ={t}^{-1}f(x,y)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, equation II is *not* a homogenous differential equation.\n\n- The function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{y}{2x}\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{x^2}{y^2}\\right)}$ is a homogeneous function of degree zero:\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{ty}{2(tx)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{(tx)^{2}}{(ty)^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\frac{t\\cdot y}{t\\cdot 2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}{x}^{2}}{{t}^{2}{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =f(x,y)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, equation III is a homogenous differential equation.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322706 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8edbecf-59d3-5f32-a0ac-d6e8f2128dc0" :knowledge-point/title "Transforming a Homogeneous Differential Equation into a Separable Equation Using a Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98e21887-70b6-5d9f-a59a-65c42892d51a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{v})+v})\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322708} {:answer/id #uuid "ff0a5320-8873-5fc7-a010-a63c0b44d79c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{{v}^{2}})+2v})\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322709} {:answer/id #uuid "449d2319-9d7f-5572-8c57-87de7c178807" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{{v}^{2}}))\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322710} {:answer/id #uuid "6e147765-48f3-501f-b446-ad145cc52a53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{{v}^{2}})+2v)\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322711} {:answer/id #uuid "7d93d88a-9043-513a-96fc-1fbbfa5eecac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{v})+2{v}^{2})\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322712}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff0a5320-8873-5fc7-a010-a63c0b44d79c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{{v}^{2}})+2v})\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322709} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c35e0ee-e673-5d29-81a2-1d81a15b0156" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322707}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "de0afd56-302d-5546-b6ff-08ceea918dd3" :question/math-academy-id "q-14314" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}})+\\frac{3y}{x}.\n$$\n\nIf the transformation $y=v(x)x$ is applied to the differential equation, what is the resulting equation?" :question/worked-solution "Using the transformation $y=vx$ (where $v=v(x)$), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given differential equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}})+\\frac{3y}{x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{v}^{2}{x}^{2}})+\\frac{3vx}{x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{{v}^{2}})+3v \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{{v}^{2}})+2v \\\\ (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{{v}^{2}})+2v})\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087947 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8edbecf-59d3-5f32-a0ac-d6e8f2128dc0" :knowledge-point/title "Transforming a Homogeneous Differential Equation into a Separable Equation Using a Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87478308-c312-590d-830c-feaf4cb1cdce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{v + 1}{2v^{3} - v^{2}})\\frac{dv}{dx} = x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084694} {:answer/id #uuid "9487561f-2063-58ea-bc91-b5107e10b5e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{2v^{2} - v}{v + 1})\\frac{dv}{dx} = x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084695} {:answer/id #uuid "b1ad20f9-2688-55d2-9b72-dffd56a10e11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{v^{2} + v}{3v^{2} - v})\\frac{dv}{dx} = \\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084696} {:answer/id #uuid "e267c795-b678-5b29-ae42-d19aa0f83aad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{v + 1}{2v^{2} - v})\\frac{dv}{dx} = \\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084697} {:answer/id #uuid "7de5aeb5-dce7-537d-a056-e779ff6f08b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{v + 1}{v^{2} + 1})\\frac{dv}{dx} = \\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084698}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e267c795-b678-5b29-ae42-d19aa0f83aad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{v + 1}{2v^{2} - v})\\frac{dv}{dx} = \\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084697} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd82c334-5f7b-5744-9a4b-62ade8bea1d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084693}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "695b49d1-9327-503a-b244-1f7f385f59ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-14315" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3{y}^{3}}{x{y}^{2}+y{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIf the transformation $y=v(x)x$ is applied to the differential equation, what is the resulting equation?" :question/worked-solution "Using the transformation $y=vx$ (where $v=v(x)$), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given differential equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3{y}^{3}}{x{y}^{2}+y{x}^{2}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{3(vx)^{3}}{x(vx)^{2}+(vx){x}^{2}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{3{v}^{3}{x}^{3}}{v{x}^{3}(v+1)} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{3{v}^{3}\\cancel{{x}^{3}}}{v\\cancel{{x}^{3}}(v+1)} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3{v}^{2}}{v+1}-v \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3{v}^{2}}{v+1}-\\frac{v(v+1)}{v+1} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3{v}^{2}-{v}^{2}-v}{v+1} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2{v}^{2}-v}{v+1} \\\\ (\\frac{v+1}{2{v}^{2}-v})\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322713 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8edbecf-59d3-5f32-a0ac-d6e8f2128dc0" :knowledge-point/title "Transforming a Homogeneous Differential Equation into a Separable Equation Using a Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ff69b2f-8ab9-55fc-95ac-e06055ef4cb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{v}^{2}+1}{1-v-{v}^{3}}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322715} {:answer/id #uuid "4f6536c1-66ff-5a00-a09b-46708deaac9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1-3v-4{v}^{2}}{1+{v}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322716} {:answer/id #uuid "08d5a410-9b73-5792-80be-c96b14a09b04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{v}^{2}+1}{1-2v+{v}^{2}-{v}^{3}}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322717} {:answer/id #uuid "dc0ed936-8649-5ea2-b4a6-d868e8720314" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{v}^{2}+1}{1+v+4{v}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322718} {:answer/id #uuid "5402799d-4127-507b-bcf5-4fc4e363902b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{v}^{2}+1}{1-3v-4{v}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322719}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08d5a410-9b73-5792-80be-c96b14a09b04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{v}^{2}+1}{1-2v+{v}^{2}-{v}^{3}}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322717} :answer-field/id #uuid "a13ff3d7-c434-5801-8915-f8f36749bcd0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322714}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d4adf8ff-bd0d-5c23-b057-08c8deed164b" :question/math-academy-id "q-14316" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{x}^{2}-xy+{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIf the transformation $y=v(x)x$ is applied to the differential equation, what is the resulting equation?" :question/worked-solution "Using the transformation $y=vx$ (where $v=v(x)$), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given differential equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-xy+{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-x(vx)+(vx)^{2}}{(vx)^{2}+{x}^{2}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{{x}^{2}(1-v+{v}^{2})}{{x}^{2}({v}^{2}+1)} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{1-v+{v}^{2}}{{v}^{2}+1} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1-v+{v}^{2}}{{v}^{2}+1}-v \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1-v+{v}^{2}-{v}^{3}-v}{{v}^{2}+1} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1-2v+{v}^{2}-{v}^{3}}{{v}^{2}+1} \\\\ \\frac{{v}^{2}+1}{1-2v+{v}^{2}-{v}^{3}}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322720 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8c2a855-07a3-5855-9f6c-1a914953ac53" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09e4268c-79a8-54aa-bf1c-aab521c18684" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{x}^{3}-3xy+xy}{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322722} {:answer/id #uuid "e1974ae0-b2e1-56f8-bc06-da719dacb176" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{x}^{2}-x{y}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{1+{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322723} {:answer/id #uuid "ccf0814b-0865-5a1a-a668-05f9e03eed36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}}{{y}^{3}+{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322724} {:answer/id #uuid "fd40ea91-b19a-5d61-9f5e-778cf9724345" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}x-{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322725} {:answer/id #uuid "b8d94ee0-0dc8-5284-8414-c6c540b932aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{3}-{x}^{2}y+{x}^{3}}{{y}^{3}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322726}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ccf0814b-0865-5a1a-a668-05f9e03eed36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}}{{y}^{3}+{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322724} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c463f5e-1065-59e9-86e1-409018d6e4fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322721}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2c7b784c-55b7-51ac-a3da-61aa471e2821" :question/math-academy-id "q-14319" :question/problem "Which of the following is a first-order homogeneous ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "A first-order ordinary differential equation that is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y)\n$$\n\nis said to be homogeneous if it satisfies the following property:\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty)=f(x,y)\\,\n$$\n\nThe only option in which this is the case is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}}{{y}^{3}+{x}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nWe can check this using one of two methods.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nLet $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}}{{y}^{3}+{x}^{3}}.$\n\nWe see that both the numerator and denominator are homogeneous polynomials of degree $3.$ Therefore, $f(x,y)$ is a homogeneous function of degree $3-3=0.$\n\nHence, the original differential equation is homogeneous.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo check this in detail using the definition, we first write:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}}{{y}^{3}+{x}^{3}}\n$$\n\nWe can check that this is homogeneous showing that $f(tx,ty)=f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(tx)^{3}-3(tx)^{2}(ty)+(tx)(ty)^{2}}{(ty)^{3}+(tx)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{3}({x}^{3})-{t}^{3}(3{x}^{2}y)+{t}^{3}(x{y}^{2})}{{t}^{3}{y}^{3}+{t}^{3}{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{3}({x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})}{{t}^{3}({y}^{3}+{x}^{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}}{{y}^{3}+{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(tx,ty)=f(x,y),$ the original differential equation is homogeneous."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322727 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "49eaf082-07de-5468-a2d7-4cdfce27a553" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Homogeneous Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83f2d7f8-3ad1-5422-8b19-31f60a1b04e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}={x}^{2}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322729} {:answer/id #uuid "bd39ad16-01f7-5d31-ab99-35997a1884f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322730} {:answer/id #uuid "bc633487-0bf1-5aa3-b454-99e596fa52f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}={x}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322731} {:answer/id #uuid "b3e39a35-fedc-5e18-94c9-09f375e9c478" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}={x}^{2}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322732} {:answer/id #uuid "4c862b7a-63f1-5bcc-a1c8-4288fd52a801" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=x(-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322733}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3e39a35-fedc-5e18-94c9-09f375e9c478" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}={x}^{2}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322732} :answer-field/id #uuid "48afae7e-3aa4-57ac-a63d-e10bb01637bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322728}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8b60ebca-2ed3-5d6e-85d9-d8b6f959696f" :question/math-academy-id "q-14321" :question/problem "Find the general solution of the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{3}+x{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}y+{x}^{3}}{x{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}y}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "This is a homogeneous equation because it is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y),\n$$\n\nand we notice that all terms in the numerator and denominator of $f(x,y)$ are of order $3$ (i.e., they are cubic).\n\nUsing the transformation $y=v(x)x$, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{3}+x{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}y+{x}^{3}}{x{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}y} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{(vx)^{3}+x(vx)^{2}+{x}^{2}(vx)+{x}^{3}}{x(vx)^{2}+{x}^{2}(vx)} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{{x}^{3}({v}^{3}+{v}^{2}+v+1)}{{x}^{3}({v}^{2}+v)} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{{v}^{3}+{v}^{2}+v+1}{{v}^{2}+v} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{v}^{3}+{v}^{2}+v+1}{{v}^{2}+v}-v \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{v}^{3}+{v}^{2}+v+1-{v}^{3}-{v}^{2}}{{v}^{2}+v} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{v+1}{v(v+1)} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{v} \\\\ v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int v\\,\\text{d}v & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{{v}^{2}}{2} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+{C}_{1} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+2{C}_{1} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we introduced $C=2{C}_{1}.$\n\nFrom the transformation $y=vx$, we get\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{y}{x}\\,.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above into our solution, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={x}^{2}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322734 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8c2a855-07a3-5855-9f6c-1a914953ac53" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14ca282c-37e1-59ec-8fd7-c4cbe5347936" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}+x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322736} {:answer/id #uuid "442138ad-837f-5e1c-bec0-3f41d04a9e51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}-x}{{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322737} {:answer/id #uuid "d697354e-8a56-58da-96c2-3635505d969f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322738} {:answer/id #uuid "190f4372-c897-5083-a24e-307e2c834ca2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{y-{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322739} {:answer/id #uuid "72f9d622-90d7-523a-a76b-14f44aee31b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{y-{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322740}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d697354e-8a56-58da-96c2-3635505d969f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322738} :answer-field/id #uuid "242eec18-5d8e-59e6-aba1-35057e4c3f5d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322735}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "29260781-8760-5b93-aff1-74357dc9fb2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-8369" :question/problem "Which of the following is a first-order homogeneous ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "A first-order ordinary differential equation that is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y)\n$$\n\nis said to be homogeneous if it satisfies the following property:\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty)=f(x,y)\\,\n$$\n\nThe only option in which this is the case is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe can check this using one of two methods.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nLet $f(x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}}.$\n\nWe see that both the numerator and denominator are homogeneous polynomials of degree $2.$ Therefore, $f(x,y)$ is a homogeneous function of degree $2-2=0.$\n\nHence, the original differential equation is homogeneous.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo check this in detail using the definition, we first write:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\n$$\n\nWe can check that this is homogeneous showing that $f(tx,ty)=f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(ty)^{2}-(tx)^{2}}{(ty)^{2}+(tx)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}{y}^{2}-{t}^{2}{x}^{2}}{{t}^{2}{y}^{2}+{t}^{2}{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})}{{t}^{2}({y}^{2}+{x}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(tx,ty)=f(x,y),$ the original differential equation is homogeneous."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088381 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8c2a855-07a3-5855-9f6c-1a914953ac53" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b387a19d-7cf7-5c7c-b4a0-3c531f2dc207" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{x^{2}y}{x^{2} + y^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087217} {:answer/id #uuid "c5fc4204-9ff1-5434-b8c8-b243a9b5a4ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{2xy}{x^{2} + y^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087218} {:answer/id #uuid "e8891a45-78f7-5a8b-a02d-8cc9d4f8af08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{x^{2}y^{2}}{x^{2} + y^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087219} {:answer/id #uuid "37572047-280a-508a-ac11-6a5fe1712a8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{xy^{2}}{x^{2} + y^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087220} {:answer/id #uuid "7253b068-0bec-5847-aaa9-da6e7449baf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{xy}{x + y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087221}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5fc4204-9ff1-5434-b8c8-b243a9b5a4ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{2xy}{x^{2} + y^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087218} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc06f993-8bb8-5bba-9060-505b57e636e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087216}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "32964a8b-4376-5c16-9f30-00e530a869bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-8739" :question/problem "Which of the following is a first-order homogeneous ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "A first-order ordinary differential equation that is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y)\n$$\n\nis said to be homogeneous if it satisfies the following property:\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty)=f(x,y)\\,\n$$\n\nThe only option in which this is the case is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe can check this using one of two methods.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nLet $f(x,y)=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$\n\nWe see that both the numerator and denominator are homogeneous polynomials of degree $2.$ Therefore, $f(x,y)$ is a homogeneous function of degree $2-2=0.$\n\nHence, the original differential equation is homogeneous.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo check this in detail using the definition, we first write:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nWe can check that this is homogeneous showing that $f(tx,ty)=f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{2(tx)(ty)}{(tx)^{2}+(ty)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}\\cdot 2xy}{{t}^{2}{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\frac{{t}^{2}\\cdot 2xy}{{t}^{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(tx,ty)=f(x,y),$ the original differential equation is homogeneous."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088382 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8c2a855-07a3-5855-9f6c-1a914953ac53" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87971271-4d9c-5148-b3f3-1acf321db5ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\ln y - \\ln x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087223} {:answer/id #uuid "936de1db-5ead-5221-8763-8a6f4805dd21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087224} {:answer/id #uuid "b4487aa0-cf0d-5d85-a6d9-c074d3f1ab09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087225} {:answer/id #uuid "bfceb5b2-39ab-518b-a918-f750fa8a0fc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\arctan (\\frac{x}{y})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087226} {:answer/id #uuid "24ac0309-cf10-5637-9bcf-a86d99411716" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} = x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087227}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24ac0309-cf10-5637-9bcf-a86d99411716" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} = x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087227} :answer-field/id #uuid "39550c0d-6eec-516e-a844-c07d2d66ac51" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087222}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8c38fa75-d73d-55f8-bbc1-1dea4b79194b" :question/math-academy-id "q-8741" :question/problem "Which of the following is NOT a first-order homogeneous ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "A first-order ordinary differential equation that is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y)\n$$\n\nis said to be homogeneous if it satisfies the following property:\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty)=f(x,y)\\,\n$$\n\nThe only option in which this is NOT the case is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=x.\n$$\n\nWe can check this using one of two methods.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nLet $f(x,y)=x.$\n\nWe see that it is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, $f(x,y)$ is a homogeneous function of degree $1.$\n\nHence, the original differential equation is  NOT homogeneous.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo check this in detail using the definition, we first write:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x\\,\n$$\n\nWe can check that this is not homogeneous showing that $f(tx,ty)\\ne f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =tx=tf(x,y)\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(tx,ty)\\ne f(x,y),$ the original differential equation is NOT homogeneous."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088383 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8edbecf-59d3-5f32-a0ac-d6e8f2128dc0" :knowledge-point/title "Transforming a Homogeneous Differential Equation into a Separable Equation Using a Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec586296-df27-5f73-bbd3-9db6ebbe9423" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{v+\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{1}{v})}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322742} {:answer/id #uuid "ac871b9d-852e-5be0-8ac3-f3091ce694da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{1}{v})}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322743} {:answer/id #uuid "f752bb46-e56c-54c2-b71f-905e661da16c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{1}{v})\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322744} {:answer/id #uuid "b8012b6a-0b93-5163-ac62-132c3f364316" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{1}{v})\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322745} {:answer/id #uuid "e6c57563-fcbc-5ec9-a911-d06d5bf24720" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2v+\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{1}{v})}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322746}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8012b6a-0b93-5163-ac62-132c3f364316" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{1}{v})\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322745} :answer-field/id #uuid "891dcd85-6ba6-5872-a203-1e2e2038527b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322741}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6aa0e045-65b8-5bd8-8a66-10e8e133beb0" :question/math-academy-id "q-8745" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{y})+\\frac{y}{x}.\n$$\n\nIf the transformation $y=v(x)x$ is applied to the differential equation, what is the resulting equation?" :question/worked-solution "Using the transformation $y=vx$ (where $v=v(x)$), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given differential equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{y})+\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{vx})+\\frac{vx}{x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{1}{v})+v \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{1}{v}) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{1}{v})} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{1}{v})\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322747 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8edbecf-59d3-5f32-a0ac-d6e8f2128dc0" :knowledge-point/title "Transforming a Homogeneous Differential Equation into a Separable Equation Using a Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3c4bd59-d8a5-5d14-b03a-e27898e0a0cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡v}-v}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322749} {:answer/id #uuid "c88b7aa5-224c-5264-b2ab-b293acc5894c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡v}+3}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322750} {:answer/id #uuid "291aecb2-71a8-5607-8db3-1a7b23ab4df0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡v}-3{v}^{2})\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322751} {:answer/id #uuid "9ff96ecd-03cd-551a-9775-7ad4db15065d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡v}-5v}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322752} {:answer/id #uuid "af8a1fc3-fc1b-5753-8ee0-b05e7ebba40b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡v}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322753}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3c4bd59-d8a5-5d14-b03a-e27898e0a0cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡v}-v}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322749} :answer-field/id #uuid "1eebb0b9-f7b4-5b1b-b702-2c54d53adfbb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322748}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "59df5070-dbd8-5482-bf07-a719f7cd3b66" :question/math-academy-id "q-8747" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})}.\n$$\n\nIf the transformation $y=v(x)x$ is applied to the differential equation, what is the resulting equation?" :question/worked-solution "Using the transformation $y=vx$ (where $v=v(x)$), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given differential equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & ={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{vx}{x})} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & ={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡v} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡v}-v \\\\ \\frac{1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡v}-v}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088388 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "49eaf082-07de-5468-a2d7-4cdfce27a553" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Homogeneous Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d0745565-b7ab-5796-a081-2e46424ec253" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{2} =-x^{3} + Cx^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087259} {:answer/id #uuid "d7b1d5a4-fd99-5c6f-b68a-4d082f11c047" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{2} =-x^{2}\\ln x^{2} + Cx^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087260} {:answer/id #uuid "d9cd2778-2a05-50a8-ba1f-5b723f108297" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{2} = x\\ln (Cx^{3} + x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087261} {:answer/id #uuid "5a5d1b52-ebe2-507d-9303-9e229260de7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{2} = x\\ln x + Cx^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087262} {:answer/id #uuid "7ec3a65d-b7cc-54bc-bedf-f31eb32640f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{3} = x^{2}\\ln (x^{2} + Cx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087263}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7b1d5a4-fd99-5c6f-b68a-4d082f11c047" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{2} =-x^{2}\\ln x^{2} + Cx^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087260} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ef6d8a2-5d7c-511b-8c0a-8a924bf134a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087258}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "19530196-afb6-5c10-98a9-988afc75a28c" :question/math-academy-id "q-8844" :question/problem "Find the general solution of the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{xy}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "This is a homogeneous equation because it is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y),\n$$\n\nand we notice that all terms in the numerator and denominator of $f(x,y)$ are of order $2$ (i.e., they are quadratic).\n\nUsing the transformation $y=v(x)x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{xy} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{(vx)^{2}-{x}^{2}}{x(vx)} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{{x}^{2}({v}^{2}-1)}{v{x}^{2}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{v}^{2}-1}{v}-v \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{v}^{2}-1-{v}^{2}}{v} \\\\ v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int v\\,\\text{d}v & =-\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{{v}^{2}}{2} & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+{C}_{1} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+2{C}_{1} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we introduced $C=2{C}_{1}.$\n\nFrom the transformation $y=vx$, we get\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{y}{x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above into our solution, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{y}{x})}^{2} & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2}+C \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2}+C{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322754 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8c2a855-07a3-5855-9f6c-1a914953ac53" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91c4cf49-08d9-5786-9604-ed9b592f0f25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+xy}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322756} {:answer/id #uuid "18f8b35a-1ace-54e1-9d26-968183cdea42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2y})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322757} {:answer/id #uuid "5c1165d8-e0d1-5593-a01c-c4e5d4ab80ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322758} {:answer/id #uuid "0b792766-334a-510d-afb2-9df445a1504a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322759} {:answer/id #uuid "f144672b-302e-5dab-b572-9c643d8e04fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{y}^{3}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322760}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b792766-334a-510d-afb2-9df445a1504a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322759} :answer-field/id #uuid "2dba84e5-66b0-51da-b273-8ce153009b18" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322755}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "02b38e2b-182e-5e7d-8ad7-efb6ba37c64c" :question/math-academy-id "q-90597" :question/problem "Which of the following is NOT a first-order homogeneous ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "A first-order ordinary differential equation that is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y)\n$$\n\nis said to be homogeneous if it satisfies the following property:\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty)=f(x,y)\\,\n$$\n\nThe only option in which this is NOT the case is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can check this using one of two methods.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nLet $f(x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}.$\n\nWe see that it is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, $f(x,y)$ is a homogeneous function of degree $2.$\n\nHence, the original differential equation is  NOT homogeneous.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo check this in detail using the definition, we first write:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\,\n$$\n\nWe can check that this is not homogeneous showing that $f(tx,ty)\\ne f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(ty)^{2}}{2} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}f(x,y)\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(tx,ty)\\ne f(x,y),$ the original differential equation is NOT homogeneous."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322761 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "49eaf082-07de-5468-a2d7-4cdfce27a553" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Homogeneous Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5100e614-3d32-5529-83fd-dfcfbfbd4f94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{C-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2})}={x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322763} {:answer/id #uuid "0097ea7d-1374-535a-bcbf-8a0e6d34897a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{C+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2})}=2{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322764} {:answer/id #uuid "c0e3dccc-1dad-5a78-95e7-17990ca580cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{4}}{C-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2})}=2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322765} {:answer/id #uuid "515c11cd-45c3-53e8-9a7c-83cae1d95144" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{C-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2})}=4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322766} {:answer/id #uuid "cfefebce-4815-58ca-ba3d-31f95d8a4682" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{C+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2})}={x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322767}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5100e614-3d32-5529-83fd-dfcfbfbd4f94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{C-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2})}={x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322763} :answer-field/id #uuid "aba66406-4955-50e7-a669-cc484689a76e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322762}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dce3c9c6-345d-5b0b-83e8-80c875dff755" :question/math-academy-id "q-90688" :question/problem "Use the transformation $y=v(x)x$ to find the general solution of the following homogeneous differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{3}}{x{y}^{2}+{x}^{3}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the transformation $y=v(x)x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{3}}{x{y}^{2}+{x}^{3}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{(vx)^{3}}{x(vx)^{2}+{x}^{3}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{{v}^{3}{x}^{3}}{({v}^{2}+1){x}^{3}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{{v}^{3}}{{v}^{2}+1} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{v}^{3}}{{v}^{2}+1}-v \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{v}^{3}-{v}^{3}-v}{{v}^{2}+1} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{v}{{v}^{2}+1} \\\\ \\frac{{v}^{2}+1}{v}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{v}^{2}+1}{v}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int (v+\\frac{1}{v})\\,\\text{d}v & =-\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{{v}^{2}}{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|v| & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+{C}_{1} \\\\ {v}^{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|v| & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+2{C}_{1} \\\\ {v}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({v}^{2}) & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we introduced $C=2{C}_{1}.$\n\nFrom the transformation $y=vx$, we get\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{y}{x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above into our solution, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{y}{x})}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡{(\\frac{y}{x})}^{2} & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})+C \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}) & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})+C \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}) & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})+C \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}) & =C \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} & =C-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{C-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2})} & ={x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322768 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "49eaf082-07de-5468-a2d7-4cdfce27a553" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Homogeneous Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "961066fa-4c79-5a20-b5ca-b8e9074b4f8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x{(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322770} {:answer/id #uuid "3dd5f8e4-5c74-5671-9519-d27f02139942" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x\\sqrt[3]{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322771} {:answer/id #uuid "e0a2f70f-8de8-5842-851d-a0a6db439e3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322772} {:answer/id #uuid "9e41ad29-590b-572d-ae11-c3ee5b9b63e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{2}(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322773} {:answer/id #uuid "af26cd02-707e-5f19-bcdd-10c5f7a95bb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322774}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3dd5f8e4-5c74-5671-9519-d27f02139942" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x\\sqrt[3]{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322771} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee04de02-8d8d-5d80-9526-285e6128e8d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322769}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9c7628a4-6ce5-5421-9e03-8195e780eaca" :question/math-academy-id "q-90691" :question/problem "Use the transformation $y=v(x)x$ to find the general solution of the following homogeneous differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}{x{y}^{2}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "With $y=v(x)x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}{x{y}^{2}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{{x}^{3}+(vx)^{3}}{x(vx)^{2}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{{x}^{3}(1+{v}^{3})}{{v}^{2}{x}^{3}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{1+{v}^{3}}{{v}^{2}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{1}{{v}^{2}}+v \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{{v}^{2}} \\\\ {v}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {v}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}v & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{{v}^{3}}{3}\\,\\text{d}v & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+{C}_{1} \\\\ {v}^{3} & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+3{C}_{1} \\\\ {v}^{3} & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we have let $C=3{C}_{1}.$\n\nFrom the transformation $y=vx$, we get\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{y}{x}\\,.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our solution, we finally obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{y}{x})}^{3} & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{3}}{{x}^{3}} & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C \\\\ {y}^{3} & ={x}^{3}(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C) \\\\ y & =x\\sqrt[3]{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322775 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "49eaf082-07de-5468-a2d7-4cdfce27a553" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Homogeneous Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96b473c7-b5f9-59c4-8ce2-b869e961e208" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=C{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322777} {:answer/id #uuid "2e2c148a-1b6d-5d0c-acfa-edfb66c104c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=C{x}^{2}+2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322778} {:answer/id #uuid "8d8123c8-0153-5fa2-a77c-450ed9e41027" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=C{x}^{3}-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322779} {:answer/id #uuid "8d4a22ff-fc4e-5e92-918f-c3c611a82c3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=C{x}^{2}-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322780} {:answer/id #uuid "931b0fc2-e492-58c6-aac5-872c3b042560" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=C{x}^{3}+x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322781}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d4a22ff-fc4e-5e92-918f-c3c611a82c3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=C{x}^{2}-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322780} :answer-field/id #uuid "805137da-dc26-5238-b8a3-91d0a7a915d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322776}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ba95a3f6-3e84-5b76-8295-96b17f255549" :question/math-academy-id "q-90692" :question/problem "Use the transformation $y=v(x)x$ to find the general solution of the following homogeneous differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{x+2y}{x}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "With $y=v(x)x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{x+2y}{x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{x+2(vx)}{x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{x(1+2v)}{x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =1+2v \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =1+v \\\\ \\frac{1}{1+v}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{1+v}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1+v}\\,\\text{d}v & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|v+1| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+{C}_{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|v+1| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{{C}_{1}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|v+1| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{{C}_{1}}|x|) \\\\ |v+1| & ={e}^{{C}_{1}}|x| \\\\ v+1 & =\\pm {e}^{{C}_{1}}x \\\\ v+1 & =Cx \\\\ v & =Cx-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we have let $C=\\pm {e}^{{C}_{1}}.$\n\nFrom the transformation $y=vx$, we get\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{y}{x}\\,.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our solution, we finally obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{x} & =Cx-1 \\\\ y & =C{x}^{2}-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
